fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1084", description: some(value: "UNIFICATION HAS NO `arrow` <-> `Function[A, B, E]` ARM, so it answers FALSE having bound NOTHING for two spellings of ONE type — and two live soundness holes ride on it.\n\nkernel-language 4.4 says outright that these are the same type: \"the stdlib surface type `Function[A, B, E]` is the *same* type — `arrow` is its shorthand (`A` = param, `B` = result, `E` = effects)\". SUBTYPING honours that: `types_compatible` carries `(Some(\"arrow\"), Some(\"parameterized\")) | (Some(\"parameterized\"), Some(\"arrow\")) => arrow_function_compatible(...)` in BOTH halves (typing.rs:37507 term-dispatch, :37604 view-structural). UNIFICATION does not: `unify_types`' dispatch has only `(Some(\"arrow\"), Some(\"arrow\"))` (typing.rs:33005, :33826). The pair falls out to the default `false`.\n\nMEASURED, `idp[A](x: A) -> A` eta-lifted and instantiated to `(x: ?A1) -> ?A1`, unified against four declared callback slots:\n\n  slot                                        unify   bindings\n  (v: Int64) -> Int64                         TRUE    1   (?A1 := Int64)\n  (v: Int64) -> String                        false   1   (?A1 := Int64, then Int64 vs String)\n  Function[A = Int64, B = Int64, E = {}]      false   0\n  Function[A = Int64, B = String, E = {}]     false   0\n\nROW 3 IS THE PROOF. That pair IS unifiable and the true answer is TRUE. So `false` there is not a verdict, it is a fall-through — unification ran, had no arm, and reported \"no\". Rows 1 and 2 are the control: the SAME question asked in the arrow spelling is answered correctly, binding `?A1` and then refusing on the result.\n\nTWO SOUNDNESS HOLES RIDE ON IT, both driven to a runtime value rather than argued (fixtures in wi1083_polytype_test as `*_is_a_known_gap` pins):\n\n  (a) RESULT TYPE. `operation callit(f: Function[A = Int64, B = String, E = {}]) -> String = f(3)` handed `idp`. No instantiation of `∀A. A -> A` gives `Int64 -> String` — the parameter pins `A := Int64` and the result then contradicts `String`. It LOADS, and `use_it()`, declared `-> String`, RETURNS `Int(3)`.\n  (b) EFFECT ROW. `operation bang[A](x: A) -> A effects {Error[T = String]} = Error.raise(\"boom\")` handed to a slot declaring `E = {}`, from a `use_it() -> Int64` declaring no effects. It LOADS and TRAPS `Raised { payload: Str(\"boom\") }`. An effect escaped a row that said `{}` — the WI-1063/WI-1078 leak shape, one indirection out.\n\nThe MONOMORPHIC twin of each is REFUSED at the same `Function[...]` slot, because a ground actual reaches `validate_arg_against_param`'s whole-type `types_compatible`, which HAS the bridge. So the hole needs BOTH conditions: a NON-GROUND actual AND the `Function[A, B, E]` spelling of the slot. That is why it surfaced on WI-1083 (an instantiated ∀ is non-ground by construction) and why WI-1083's arity row, written in the ARROW spelling, was refused while its result-type and effect-row rows were not — the split was never about arity vs result, it was about the SPELLING.\n\nDO NOT DECOMPOSE POSITIONALLY. WI-775 settled that `Function[A, ...]`'s `A` is the ARGUMENT's data type — a named-tuple `A` is ONE tuple-typed argument, not a parameter list — and `arrow_function_compatible` is the function that knows that convention. The arm must delegate to a unify-flavoured twin of it, not to `unify_arrow_view`. Getting this wrong reopens WI-775 / WI-791 / WI-792.\n\nTHE FIX MAY NOT BE SUFFICIENT ON ITS OWN, and this must be MEASURED rather than assumed. Downstream of the binding sit two further withholdings that could still swallow the verdict: `validate_arg_against_param` keeps the SHALLOW pair when `type_contains_callable` fires (so a `?A1` bound only in σ may still read as non-ground at `validate_arrow_param_result`, whose component compares are groundness-gated); and `validate_callback_effect_row` bails for (b) on a separate reason entirely — a `Function[A, B, E]` parameter declares no binder PLACES to align the actual's row against, so `actual_places.len() != declared_places.len()` returns None. Establish by measurement which of the three levels each hole actually needs.\n\nACCEPTANCE: `unify_types` answers TRUE for row 3 and FALSE-with-`?A1`-bound for row 4 (the arrow rows keep their verdicts — they are the control); the two `*_is_a_known_gap` pins in wi1083_polytype_test become REFUSALS and are rewritten as such, with their monomorphic twins kept beside them; both runtime demonstrations above are refused at LOAD; WI-775/791/792's `Function`-slot admissions stay green (`wi787_eta_spread_named_tuple_test`, `wi784_closure_arity_test`, `wi775*`, `wi792*` are the named non-regressions); a test says which case fails when each level is backed out separately; every seven-tier corpus load keeps its fact/rule totals. cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nREFERENCE: WI-1083 (where this surfaced — the ∀ that makes an argument non-ground, and the two pins), WI-775 (what `Function`'s `A` means), WI-791/WI-792 (arrow arity and the function-value argument check), WI-385 (the groundness discipline the second level belongs to), WI-440 (`validate_callback_effect_row`), WI-420 (`attach_eta_dispatch_dict`, which works around this by unifying the PARAM parts rather than the whole types — its comment is where the non-decomposition is recorded), kernel-language 4.4."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-12T11:28:48Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1084", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. THREE WITHHOLDINGS STACKED OVER ONE ROOT, each independently load-bearing and each measured by its own revert against the whole `anthill-core` crate:\n\n  1. `unify_arrow_function_view` + two `unify_types` dispatch arms — the missing decomposition, so the relation BINDS.   back-out cost 2\n  2. `validate_arrow_param_result` resolves the RESULT through σ before testing it for groundness.                       back-out cost 1\n  3. `validate_callback_effect_row` no longer discards the row check when there are NO PLACES to align.                  back-out cost 1\n\nTHE RESULT-TYPE HOLE NEEDED 1 AND 2 TOGETHER; THE EFFECT-ROW HOLE NEEDED 3 ALONE. That is the finding that could not be reasoned to: fix 1 on its own changed NO end-to-end verdict, which reads as \"the fix does not work\" and is in fact \"there is a second withholding behind it\". Measuring each level separately was the only way to see it, and the ticket's instruction to do so is what caught it.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY CLOSES: `callit(idp)` at `Function[A = Int64, B = String, E = {}]` is refused at the argument (it LOADED, and `use_it()` — declared `-> String` — returned `Int(3)`); `callit(bang)` at `E = {}` is refused naming the effect (\"operation `bang` declares `Error[T = String]`, which the closed row does not admit\") — it LOADED and TRAPPED `Raised { payload: Str(\"boom\") }` out of a `use_it() -> Int64` declaring no effects at all. Both monomorphic twins are asserted beside them, refused throughout, so neither row can pass on a checker that merely refuses more.\n\nTHE PARAM COMPONENT IS GATED ON ARITY, NOT GROUNDNESS — and the first cut got that wrong in a way worth recording. A `resolved_type_is_ground` gate on `A` passed the suite and was WRONG: it also refused `A ~ ?X`, a var-to-var unification the substitution handles natively (`bind_compressed`'s path compression IS the equality class), so it threw away legitimate inference to dodge a problem that is not about variables. The real rule is WI-791's: at arrow arity 1 the `param` IS the sole parameter's type, the same category as `A`, and they unify freely; at any other arity it is a parameter LIST, related to `A` only by the WI-775/787 SPREAD convention — a rule about how a CALL is written, not a type identity — so binding it is wrong. MEASURED: without the gate, `wi787_eta_spread_named_tuple_test` fails in 4 rows at a SIBLING argument (`expected (_1: Int64, _2: Int64), got (x: Int64, acc: Int64)`) — the slot's own `A` pinned to the positional spelling and then read by the next parameter, where a data tuple is name-keyed.\n\nTHE PARAM IS DELIBERATELY NOT σ-RESOLVED IN LEVEL 2, and that is its own finding rather than caution. Resolving it makes both sides ground for an ordinary higher-order call, and `validate_arrow_param_result`'s BY-NAME comparison then refuses a pairing WI-782 requires: `foldLeft(f: (acc: Acc, x: Element) -> Acc)` given an eta arrow `(_1: Int64, _2: Int64) -> Int64`, 38 tests across WI-064/278/411/424/492/493/585. The by-name rule is right for the `Function[A = …]` spelling this function also serves and WRONG for a genuine arrow-vs-arrow parameter list, which must align positionally — the groundness gate has been hiding that disagreement. Recorded at the site; splitting the two readings is a separate change with its own measurement.\n\nONE JUDGEMENT GENUINELY CHANGES, and it is pinned rather than left to be discovered (code review): `(arrow, parameterized)` used to be a form MISMATCH and took `unify_types`' `_ =>` fallback, which is `types_compatible` — so for this pair unify was literally equal to SUBTYPING and effects compared covariantly. With an arm it means what it means everywhere else, EQUALITY, as the `(arrow, arrow)` arm always has. `the_arms_make_unify_stricter_than_the_subtype_fallback_it_replaced` asserts both relations on one pair so they are seen to disagree ON PURPOSE. Three callers act on the verdict rather than discarding it — `constrain_vid` (marks the substitution contradictory), `hint_instantiation_subst` (drops the pin), `unify_parameterized_view` (fails a binding set); the argument path is not among them.\n\nA TEST THAT WAS NOT RUNNING, caught here and worth the memory: rewriting the result-type row from a known-gap pin into a refusal dropped its `#[test]` attribute, so it silently stopped being a test and \"passed\" by not existing — which also made the level-1 back-out look cheap (1 row instead of 2). Found only by dumping the refusal MESSAGES instead of trusting a green suite; the messages are now asserted, naming both the slot and the contradicted type.\n\nALSO FROM REVIEW: `instantiate_poly_type` returned `None` for the WHOLE elimination instead of `continue`-ing past a malformed binder (a `debug_assert!` + `continue` is silent in release, and a dropped binder leaves the variable SHARED — the exact aliasing the design retires); the malformed-∀ error is now raised BEFORE `attach_eta_dispatch_dict`, whose own `poly_type_body` read is likewise `None` there and would have reported `UnsatisfiableRequirement` (WI-420) instead; `decode_cons_cell` routes through `is_list_cons_cell` so the spine predicate has one owner.\n\nMEASURED: whole workspace green via `rustland/scripts/test.sh` (exit 0, 35 binaries). All seven corpus tiers load with ZERO errors and unchanged totals — `anthill-todo`'s +2 against the WI-1083 baseline is bookkeeping, verified by stashing `workitems.anthill` and re-loading (this ticket's own fact is exactly +1, the other is WI-1083's delivery `Feedback`). No stdlib change, so scaland is untouched.", at: "2026-08-12T11:28:48Z")
